There's no `.csproj` yet, so I can't confirm the target framework. Here's the lightest option depending on your .NET version:

---

### .NET 8+ (recommended): Use the built-in `TimeProvider`

`TimeProvider` is an abstract class in the BCL — no packages needed. Inject it via constructor:

```csharp
public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _time;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? time = null)
        => _time = time ?? TimeProvider.System;

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _time.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

In tests, use `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` (one small NuGet) or just subclass `TimeProvider` yourself:

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

Production callers just do `new InvoiceCalculator()` — no DI container needed.

---

### Pre-.NET 8: Minimal `IClock` interface

```csharp
public interface IClock { DateTimeOffset UtcNow { get; } }

public class SystemClock : IClock
{
    public DateTimeOffset UtcNow => DateTimeOffset.UtcNow;
}
```

Same constructor-defaulting pattern applies.

---

**TL;DR**: A constructor parameter with a default of `TimeProvider.System` (or `new SystemClock()`) is the lightest change — no DI container, no ambient static, fully testable. Want me to apply this to your `InvoiceCalculator`?
